00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,920
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:02,180
We meet again.

3
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:02,380
Ah...

4
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:05,420
So today, let's talk about how video platforms are actually built.

5
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:08,240
or multimedia platforms, are actually built.

6
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,270
So, when we're just scrolling through TikTok or Bilibili in our daily lives,

7
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:14,310
But there's a question that rarely crosses most people's minds.

8
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:16,690
These platforms have to deal with users every single day.

9
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:20,220
millions, or even tens of millions of video uploads.

10
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,080
So, how exactly does it pull that off?

11
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:23,770
And it's not just about the uploads.

12
00:00:23,770 --> 00:00:26,390
And on top of that, it has to ensure that a massive number of users

13
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:29,610
to watch videos online smoothly at the same time.

14
00:00:29,610 --> 00:00:31,090
So, how can we break this problem down?

15
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:35,080
So, at its core, this is a complex problem of distributed systems.

16
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:36,120
So,

17
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:38,760
So, at its core, there are four key things we absolutely have to tackle.

18
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,760
So, the first thing is handling the production of large files.

19
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:44,430
I mean, these files can easily be several, or even tens of gigabytes.

20
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:47,110
So, that's why we absolutely need this chunked upload.

21
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:48,550
and resumable uploads.

22
00:00:48,550 --> 00:00:50,700
This ensures a good user experience.

23
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,640
Alright, next up, we have transcoding.

24
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,860
So, a single video needs to be processed into multiple resolutions.

25
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:57,210
or in various formats.

26
00:00:57,210 --> 00:01:00,230
This is to accommodate different devices and network conditions.

27
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:02,490
Alright, the next step is storage.

28
00:01:02,490 --> 00:01:05,860
So, the storage system doesn't just hold our original videos.

29
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:07,140
but also the output from the transcoding process.

30
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:09,200
All these videos in different resolutions have to be stored.

31
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,160
So, we're talking about a storage scale that's definitely in the petabyte range.

32
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:13,120
So, we're definitely talking about a petabyte-scale storage system.

33
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:14,560
And finally, we have playback.

34
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,920
It needs to adjust based on the user's device and their network conditions.

35
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,620
and dynamically switch the resolution.

36
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:24,480
And this is what we often call adaptive streaming.

37
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:26,080
So, looking at the big picture,

38
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,240
So, the core capabilities of this system...

39
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:30,310
essentially, upload and transcoding,

40
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:31,090
and storage.

41
00:01:31,090 --> 00:01:32,710
and, of course, playback.

42
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:35,360
So, building on that foundation, let's break it down further.

43
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:40,900
And that other category is, of course, the non-functional requirements.

44
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:43,680
For instance, supporting large file uploads.

45
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,890
And then, you have chunked uploads and resumable transfers.

46
00:01:46,890 --> 00:01:47,710
Alright, so for the other category...

47
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:51,830
And for the others, we're talking about supporting the conversion of videos into multiple formats and resolutions.

48
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:52,930
Right.

49
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:53,950
like 4K, 1080P,

50
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:55,050
Yeah.

51
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:56,790
720P, 720P,

52
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:58,250
Of course, there are other protocols as well.

53
00:01:58,250 --> 00:02:02,570
For example, protocols like HLS and DASH.

54
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:04,810
Next up, we have the video's metadata.

55
00:02:04,810 --> 00:02:07,270
For instance, a video like this one definitely has a title.

56
00:02:07,270 --> 00:02:08,130
its tags,

57
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:10,190
its thumbnails,

58
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:11,540
and the thumbnail image.

59
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:14,990
All of this is managed by the video's metadata.

60
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:16,970
And then, of course, there are our functional requirements.

61
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:19,800
It also needs to support the upload of tens of millions of videos every single day.

62
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:23,760
It also needs to support tens of millions of users watching videos online simultaneously.

63
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:25,720
So, those are our functional requirements.

64
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:27,220
So, what about the non-functional requirements?

65
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:28,540
So basically, it needs to be highly available, right?

66
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:29,540
There can be no single point of failure.

67
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:30,560
It needs to be low-latency.

68
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:31,780
It needs to be fault-tolerant.

69
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,130
And it needs to be scalable.

70
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:35,390
And then there's the data.

71
00:02:35,390 --> 00:02:36,630
We're aiming for eventual consistency.

72
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:38,350
So it's definitely this kind of system.

73
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:42,280
Not necessarily. In fact, we definitely wouldn't build it as a strongly consistent system.

74
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:43,580
Okay.

75
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:48,100
Okay, so those are the requirements and challenges our video platform is facing.

76
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:52,080
Alright, so next up, we actually need to bring this system to life.

77
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:53,660
So, we need to break this down into services.

78
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:57,019
So, let's take a look at how we're going to break down this service.

79
00:02:57,019 --> 00:03:00,739
What I mean is, we can't just cram all the logic and business rules into a single project.

80
00:03:00,739 --> 00:03:03,319
or within a single service.

81
00:03:03,319 --> 00:03:04,559
It absolutely has to be split up.

82
00:03:04,559 --> 00:03:07,439
So, generally speaking, for video platforms like Bilibili or TikTok,

83
00:03:07,439 --> 00:03:09,430
So, when we break down the core components,

84
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:11,230
So, we'll break it down into four main modules.

85
00:03:11,230 --> 00:03:13,170
Alright, so the first one is our upload service.

86
00:03:13,170 --> 00:03:14,570
And the second one is transcoding.

87
00:03:14,570 --> 00:03:15,970
And the third one is video management.

