89
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:21,810
Alright, so first, let's take a look at the upload service.

90
00:03:21,810 --> 00:03:24,290
So, the upload service is actually pretty straightforward.

91
00:03:24,290 --> 00:03:25,650
First, there's the entry point for file uploads.

92
00:03:25,650 --> 00:03:27,760
And then there's the file verification.

93
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:29,320
And then there's our shard upload.

94
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:30,200
And resumable transfers.

95
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:31,400
and merging the chunks.

96
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:34,980
So, for shard upload, it's basically about taking a large file,

97
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:36,959
and breaks it down into many smaller chunks.

98
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:38,899
And then, each piece is uploaded individually.

99
00:03:38,899 --> 00:03:40,259
So, once the upload is finished,

100
00:03:40,259 --> 00:03:42,399
The system will then merge them.

101
00:03:42,399 --> 00:03:45,130
merging these smaller chunks back into one large file.

102
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:47,290
And that's how you get one complete file.

103
00:03:47,290 --> 00:03:48,410
And then there's the resume from breakpoint feature.

104
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:50,950
So, let's say the user refreshes the webpage.

105
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:54,360
or, uh, closes the browser.

106
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:55,760
So, the next time they come back,

107
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,520
If it finds that there's an identical file here,

108
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:00,120
the upload can be resumed.

109
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,020
Okay, next up, let's look at the transcoding task.

110
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:03,960
So, what about the transcoding task?

111
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,640
So basically, it takes the original video that was uploaded,

112
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:07,800
into different resolutions.

113
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:10,380
into different formats, each as a separate task.

114
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:15,300
And actually, this is a key point that we'll be covering in this series of videos.

115
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:19,630
This is one of the main, key points we'll be covering.

116
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:21,630
So, as you're learning about the transcoding tasks,

117
00:04:21,630 --> 00:04:23,340
you really need to pay close attention and learn it well.

118
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,100
So, its main function is transcoding into multiple formats.

119
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:27,660
transcoding to multiple resolutions and adjusting video quality.

120
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:32,260
And later on, uh, in the video that covers the transcoding task,

121
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:34,850
I'll also walk you through that in detail.

122
00:04:34,850 --> 00:04:35,930
And then, we have the video service.

123
00:04:35,930 --> 00:04:37,170
Basically, it's all about managing the metadata for our videos.

124
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:38,930
Ah,

125
00:04:38,930 --> 00:04:41,090
And then, for example, things like the video's title,

126
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:42,400
the thumbnail,

127
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,420
And then, for the 'My Videos' list,

128
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,800
And also, for example, the video list on the homepage.

129
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,200
All of these are provided through interfaces from the video service.

130
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,909
It also provides search capabilities.

131
00:04:52,269 --> 00:04:54,029
Okay, so that's our streaming service.

132
00:04:54,029 --> 00:04:59,229
So, when we're on this page and click on a video,

133
00:04:59,229 --> 00:05:03,089
So, what's actually happening is, it's not playing the original video file directly.

134
00:05:03,089 --> 00:05:04,690
So, what's actually happening is,

135
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:07,190
What it actually does is pull the resources in different resolutions.

136
00:05:07,190 --> 00:05:08,290
So, for those resources in different resolutions,

137
00:05:08,290 --> 00:05:10,600
and it will return the address for that specific resolution.

138
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:13,060
And then, the player on our web page...

139
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:15,200
or the player in the app will start playing it.

140
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,160
It can automatically switch between these different resolutions.

141
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:19,680
to ensure a smooth playback experience.

142
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:23,409
So, these are the main tasks our streaming service needs to handle.

143
00:05:23,809 --> 00:05:25,980
Okay, so we've broken down the services.

144
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:26,740
So, now that we have these services,

145
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:29,340
So, let's take a look at how our system is actually layered.

146
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:31,300
Alright, so let's start from the outermost layer.

147
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:32,140
Which is the client side.

148
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:34,500
This includes our mobile app, web, and H5 clients.

149
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:36,900
So, all of these requests will be sent to our gateway layer.

150
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:38,400
This is our API entry layer.

151
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,840
So, this API entry point is what we call the gateway layer.

152
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,340
Its main job is to handle things like parameter validation,

153
00:05:43,340 --> 00:05:44,080
routing, and authentication.

154
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:45,659
Right.

155
00:05:45,659 --> 00:05:46,859
So, to dive a bit deeper...

156
00:05:46,859 --> 00:05:49,550
There might also be some operations related to rate limiting.

157
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:52,790
Okay, so right below the API gateway, we have our service layer.

158
00:05:52,790 --> 00:05:53,870
Take our user service, for example.

159
00:05:53,870 --> 00:05:54,850
It handles things like registration,

160
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:56,150
login, right?

161
00:05:56,150 --> 00:05:58,210
updating some user information,

162
00:05:58,210 --> 00:06:00,570
And then, we have our upload service.

163
00:06:00,570 --> 00:06:02,690
So, this is our upload service for all files.

164
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:04,490
All file uploads go through this service.

165
00:06:04,490 --> 00:06:05,730
all go through our upload service.

166
00:06:05,730 --> 00:06:07,170
And then there's our video service.

167
00:06:07,170 --> 00:06:09,790
This is the metadata for our videos.

168
00:06:09,790 --> 00:06:12,450
For example, things like the video list are all handled by our video service and streaming media service.

169
00:06:12,450 --> 00:06:13,390
And then,

170
00:06:13,390 --> 00:06:17,020
And for video playback, that's all handled by our dedicated streaming media service.

171
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:19,900
And then, going even deeper, we have our middleware.

172
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:20,620
layers.

173
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:22,260
For instance, we need caching, right?

174
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:23,250
we'll need Redis.

175
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:26,250
So, for our transcoding tasks, we definitely need to execute them asynchronously.

176
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:29,310
So, we'll use RocketMQ to handle this asynchronous transcoding.

177
00:06:29,310 --> 00:06:32,310
Of course, you could also use something like Kafka.

178
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:33,370
or other MQs.

179
00:06:33,370 --> 00:06:35,350
And then, of course, we have our file system.

180
00:06:35,350 --> 00:06:37,120
So, for our files, they need to be stored.

181
00:06:37,120 --> 00:06:39,960
Alright, so now, for our entire project...

182
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:41,120
We're using MinIO for this.

183
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:42,760
Of course, you could also use Alibaba Cloud's OSS.

184
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:45,480
or any other OSS solutions would work just fine.

185
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:48,200
And then we have NACOS.

186
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,700
It's mainly used for our service registration and discovery.

187
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:53,660
And this is what we call the entire service governance.

188
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:57,710
So, we're using NACOS here as our service registry and configuration center.

189
00:06:57,710 --> 00:07:01,830
And then, we also have what's called the task layer.

190
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:05,459
So, the task layer is primarily responsible for our transcoding tasks.

191
00:07:05,459 --> 00:07:07,790
Of course, there are also some other auxiliary tasks.

192
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:09,350
For instance, consuming messages from the message queue.

193
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:11,930
And then, there's updating the status of the task.

194
00:07:11,930 --> 00:07:13,390
and outputting the corresponding deliverables, and so on.

195
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:16,480
All of this will be handled within our task layer.

196
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:19,200
Okay, and at the very bottom, we have our data layer.

197
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,960
And that's because, of course, all of our data has to be stored in a database.

198
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:23,940
First up, we have our user data.

199
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,400
For instance, when a user registers, right?

200
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,780
So, some of the user's information needs to be stored in the user database.

201
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:31,790
And of course, our upload tasks also need to be persisted in the database.

202
00:07:31,790 --> 00:07:34,110
Because we need to implement features like chunked upload and resumable transfer, right?

203
00:07:34,110 --> 00:07:37,310
So, it's a given that we have to store certain states in the database.

204
00:07:37,310 --> 00:07:39,790
And then we have our video metadata. This includes information like, 'Which videos did I upload?' right?

205
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:41,470
So, I've uploaded some videos, right?

206
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:42,780
like what videos are in which lists.

207
00:07:42,780 --> 00:07:45,380
All of this gets stored in the video metadata database.

208
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:48,440
And finally, we have the streaming media resources.

209
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:50,260
And this data... this database.

210
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:52,660
Now, the streaming resources are basically the output from the transcoding process.

211
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:53,880
like 4K and 1080p,

212
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:57,580
and the URLs for the different resolutions.

213
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:00,030
All of these will be stored in our streaming media...

214
00:08:00,030 --> 00:08:01,770
in the database for this resource.

215
00:08:01,770 --> 00:08:04,870
Okay, so that's a wrap on the overall layered architecture.

216
00:08:04,870 --> 00:08:06,390
Alright, so next up, let's take a look at...

217
00:08:06,390 --> 00:08:09,090
This involves some of the tables.

218
00:08:09,090 --> 00:08:11,980
So, uh, because I want to give you all a general idea of this...

219
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:13,920
to first get a general understanding of the project as a whole.

220
00:08:13,920 --> 00:08:15,380
So, I won't be going into too much detail on the tables.

221
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:18,010
So, all you really need to do is remember what tables we have.

222
00:08:18,010 --> 00:08:19,370
Alright, so first up is the upload service.

223
00:08:19,370 --> 00:08:20,680
So, that's our upload service.

224
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,480
So, there must be an upload task.

225
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:24,800
So, of course, these upload tasks have to be stored in the database.

226
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:26,140
And then there's the 'upload_part'.

227
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:27,880
And then there's the 'upload_part'...

228
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:30,540
Because, for a single upload task,

229
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,340
So basically, this corresponds to one of our upload_tasks.

230
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:35,700
So, for the chunked upload, all the information about the chunks...

231
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:38,299
will be stored in this 'upload_part' table.

232
00:08:38,299 --> 00:08:40,179
Next up, we have the transcoding domain.

233
00:08:40,179 --> 00:08:42,379
So, the transcoding process is divided into a main transcoding task,

234
00:08:42,379 --> 00:08:44,019
and the transcoding subtasks.

